const t=[{id:"bicycle",title:"The Bicycle Comes Back",topic:"交通和城市",minutes:15,level:"base",paragraphs:[["A","In the 1950s, the bicycle was the most common form of transport in many European cities. Factory workers cycled to work, children rode to school and shopkeepers used bicycles with large baskets to deliver goods. As incomes rose in the following decades, however, families bought cars, and city planners built wider roads and car parks. By the 1970s, cycling had become something that most adults did only on holiday."],["B","The first city to reverse this trend was Amsterdam. After a series of road accidents in which many children were killed, parents organised protests that demanded safer streets. The city responded by building separate cycle lanes and lowering speed limits in residential areas. Today, more than a third of all journeys in the city are made by bicycle."],["C","Other cities have followed, but often for different reasons. In Paris, the main motivation was air pollution. The city created hundreds of kilometres of new lanes and closed a busy road beside the River Seine to cars. In Bogotá, the capital of Colombia, roads are closed to traffic every Sunday so that residents can cycle, walk or skate freely."],["D","Bicycle-sharing schemes have also helped. Users can pick up a bicycle at one station and leave it at another, paying only for the time they use it. Electric bicycles, which make hills and long distances much easier, have attracted older riders who would not otherwise cycle."],["E","Not every scheme has been successful. In some cities, shared bicycles were left on pavements or thrown into rivers, and companies went out of business. Experts say that bicycles alone are not enough: people will only cycle regularly if they feel safe, and that usually means lanes that are physically separated from cars."]],groups:[{title:"Questions 1–4",instruction:"Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Choose TRUE, FALSE or NOT GIVEN.",method:"reading-2",questions:[{n:1,type:"tfng",q:"In the 1950s, some shopkeepers delivered goods by bicycle.",answer:"TRUE",explain:"A 段：shopkeepers used bicycles with large baskets to deliver goods。意思一致，选 TRUE。"},{n:2,type:"tfng",q:"Cycling became more popular as people earned more money.",answer:"FALSE",explain:"A 段：As incomes rose … families bought cars … cycling had become something that most adults did only on holiday。收入提高后骑车反而变少，和题目相反，选 FALSE。"},{n:3,type:"tfng",q:"The protests in Amsterdam were organised by parents.",answer:"TRUE",explain:"B 段：parents organised protests that demanded safer streets。选 TRUE。"},{n:4,type:"tfng",q:"Paris now has more cycle lanes than Amsterdam.",answer:"NOT GIVEN",explain:"C 段只说巴黎修了几百公里新车道，没有和阿姆斯特丹比较。原文没有这个信息，选 NOT GIVEN。比较级题目要找原文有没有同样的比较。"}]},{title:"Questions 5–7",instruction:"Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.",method:"reading-3",questions:[{n:5,type:"gap",q:"Amsterdam reduced ______ in residential areas.",answer:["speed limits"],explain:"B 段：lowering speed limits in residential areas。lowering ≈ reduced，答案 speed limits，注意复数。"},{n:6,type:"gap",q:"The main reason Paris encouraged cycling was ______.",answer:["air pollution"],explain:"C 段：In Paris, the main motivation was air pollution。main motivation ≈ main reason。"},{n:7,type:"gap",q:"Electric bicycles have encouraged ______ to start cycling.",answer:["older riders"],explain:"D 段：Electric bicycles … have attracted older riders who would not otherwise cycle。attracted ≈ encouraged … to start。"}]},{title:"Questions 8–10",instruction:"Which paragraph contains the following information? Choose the correct letter, A–E.",method:"reading-9",questions:[{n:8,type:"match",q:"an example of a city that stops cars from using roads one day a week",options:["A","B","C","D","E"],answer:"C",explain:"C 段：In Bogotá … roads are closed to traffic every Sunday。every Sunday ≈ one day a week，选 C。"},{n:9,type:"match",q:"a description of how users pay for shared bicycles",options:["A","B","C","D","E"],answer:"D",explain:"D 段：paying only for the time they use it。选 D。"},{n:10,type:"match",q:"a reason why some bicycle companies failed",options:["A","B","C","D","E"],answer:"E",explain:"E 段：shared bicycles were left on pavements or thrown into rivers, and companies went out of business。went out of business ≈ failed，选 E。"}]}]},{id:"foodwaste",title:"Food Waste at Home",topic:"环境和消费",minutes:15,level:"base",paragraphs:[["A","Around one third of all the food produced in the world is never eaten. Some is lost on farms or during transport, but in wealthy countries the largest share is thrown away in people's homes. A typical family in the United Kingdom, for example, wastes food worth about £700 a year."],["B","Researchers who have studied household bins say that the most commonly wasted items are not expensive meals but everyday foods such as bread, milk, potatoes and fresh fruit. These products have a short life, and people often buy more than they need because large packs seem cheaper."],["C",'Confusion about date labels is another cause. Many shoppers do not understand the difference between a "use by" date and a "best before" date. The first is about safety: food should not be eaten after this date. The second is about quality: food may be less fresh after this date, but it is usually still safe to eat. Some supermarkets have removed "best before" dates from fruit and vegetables so that customers judge freshness for themselves.'],["D","Simple habits can make a large difference. Writing a shopping list, checking the fridge before going to the shop and freezing leftovers can all reduce waste. In one experiment in Denmark, families who planned their meals for the week threw away about 20 percent less food than before."],["E","Wasted food also has an environmental cost. When food rots in landfill sites, it produces methane, a gas that traps far more heat than carbon dioxide. For this reason, some cities now collect food waste separately and turn it into fertiliser or energy."]],groups:[{title:"Questions 1–4",instruction:"Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Choose TRUE, FALSE or NOT GIVEN.",method:"reading-2",questions:[{n:1,type:"tfng",q:"In rich countries, most food waste happens on farms.",answer:"FALSE",explain:"A 段：in wealthy countries the largest share is thrown away in people's homes。浪费最多的是家里，不是农场，选 FALSE。"},{n:2,type:"tfng",q:"Expensive meals are the food most often thrown away.",answer:"FALSE",explain:"B 段：the most commonly wasted items are not expensive meals but everyday foods。选 FALSE。"},{n:3,type:"tfng",q:'Food should not be eaten after its "use by" date.',answer:"TRUE",explain:"C 段：The first is about safety: food should not be eaten after this date。the first 指 use by，选 TRUE。"},{n:4,type:"tfng",q:'Customers prefer fruit without "best before" dates.',answer:"NOT GIVEN",explain:"C 段只说有些超市去掉了水果蔬菜的 best before 日期，没说顾客更喜欢哪种。选 NOT GIVEN。"}]},{title:"Questions 5–7",instruction:"Choose the correct letter, A, B, C or D.",method:"reading-13",questions:[{n:5,type:"mcq",q:"According to paragraph B, why do people buy too much food?",options:["A  They forget what is in the fridge.","B  Large packs appear to be better value.","C  Shops close early in the evening.","D  Fresh food is difficult to find."],answer:"B",explain:"B 段：people often buy more than they need because large packs seem cheaper。seem cheaper ≈ appear to be better value，选 B。A 是 D 段提到的建议，不是 B 段说的原因。"},{n:6,type:"mcq",q:"What happened in the Danish experiment?",options:["A  Families who planned meals wasted less food.","B  Families bought more frozen food.","C  Families saved £700 a year.","D  Families stopped using shopping lists."],answer:"A",explain:"D 段：families who planned their meals for the week threw away about 20 percent less food。选 A。C 的 £700 是 A 段英国家庭一年浪费的金额，张冠李戴。"},{n:7,type:"mcq",q:"Why do some cities collect food waste separately?",options:["A  to sell it to farmers abroad","B  to stop people eating unsafe food","C  to reduce harmful gases from landfill","D  to make supermarkets pay for it"],answer:"C",explain:"E 段：食物在垃圾填埋场腐烂会产生 methane（甲烷），For this reason 城市单独收集。选 C。"}]},{title:"Questions 8–10",instruction:"Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD ONLY from the passage for each answer.",method:"reading-4",questions:[{n:8,type:"gap",q:"Families can waste less by writing a shopping ______,",answer:["list"],explain:"D 段：Writing a shopping list。"},{n:9,type:"gap",q:"and by ______ any food that is left over.",answer:["freezing"],explain:"D 段：freezing leftovers。空前有 by，要填动名词 freezing。"},{n:10,type:"gap",q:"Food rotting in landfill produces ______, which traps a lot of heat.",answer:["methane"],explain:"E 段：it produces methane, a gas that traps far more heat than carbon dioxide。"}]}]},{id:"yawn",title:"Why Do We Yawn?",topic:"科学和身体",minutes:15,level:"base",paragraphs:[["A","Almost every animal with a backbone yawns, from fish and birds to dogs and humans. Babies begin to yawn even before they are born. Yet, although yawning is so common, scientists still disagree about what it is for."],["B","For many years, the most popular explanation was that yawning brings more oxygen into the body. However, when researchers asked volunteers to breathe air with extra oxygen, the volunteers yawned just as often as before. Most scientists now reject the oxygen theory."],["C","A newer idea is that yawning helps to cool the brain. A deep yawn draws cool air into the mouth and increases blood flow to the head. In one study, people yawned less when they held a cold pack against their forehead. Supporters of this theory also point out that people yawn more in spring and autumn than in very hot or very cold weather."],["D","Yawning is also contagious: seeing, hearing or even reading about a yawn can make us yawn. This effect is stronger between family members and close friends than between strangers. For this reason, some psychologists believe that contagious yawning is linked to empathy, the ability to understand how other people feel."],["E","Interestingly, children usually do not catch yawns from others until they are about four years old, which is roughly the age when they start to understand other people's thoughts. Dogs, too, sometimes yawn after watching their owners yawn, but researchers are still debating whether this shows real empathy."]],groups:[{title:"Questions 1–4",instruction:"Choose the correct heading for paragraphs B–E from the list of headings below.",method:"reading-8",box:["i  A theory that has been disproved","ii  Why yawning spreads between people","iii  The best time of day to yawn","iv  A link between yawning and temperature","v  When the ability to catch yawns develops","vi  Yawning as a sign of illness"],questions:[{n:1,type:"heading",q:"Paragraph B",options:["i","ii","iii","iv","v","vi"],answer:"i",explain:"B 段讲氧气理论：实验后 Most scientists now reject the oxygen theory。被推翻的理论，选 i。"},{n:2,type:"heading",q:"Paragraph C",options:["i","ii","iii","iv","v","vi"],answer:"iv",explain:"C 段讲打哈欠给大脑降温，还提到冷敷和季节温度。和温度有关，选 iv。"},{n:3,type:"heading",q:"Paragraph D",options:["i","ii","iii","iv","v","vi"],answer:"ii",explain:"D 段：Yawning is also contagious，并解释和共情有关。讲哈欠为什么会传染，选 ii。"},{n:4,type:"heading",q:"Paragraph E",options:["i","ii","iii","iv","v","vi"],answer:"v",explain:"E 段：孩子大约四岁才会被传染哈欠。讲这种能力什么时候出现，选 v。iii、vi 文中没提。"}]},{title:"Questions 5–7",instruction:"Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Choose TRUE, FALSE or NOT GIVEN.",method:"reading-2",questions:[{n:5,type:"tfng",q:"Babies can yawn before birth.",answer:"TRUE",explain:"A 段：Babies begin to yawn even before they are born。选 TRUE。"},{n:6,type:"tfng",q:"Volunteers given extra oxygen yawned less often.",answer:"FALSE",explain:"B 段：the volunteers yawned just as often as before。次数没变少，选 FALSE。"},{n:7,type:"tfng",q:"People yawn most often in summer.",answer:"FALSE",explain:"C 段：people yawn more in spring and autumn than in very hot or very cold weather。夏天很热，打哈欠反而少，选 FALSE。"}]},{title:"Questions 8–10",instruction:"Complete the sentences below. Choose ONE WORD ONLY from the passage for each answer.",method:"reading-3",questions:[{n:8,type:"gap",q:"Holding a cold ______ against the forehead made people yawn less.",answer:["pack"],explain:"C 段：people yawned less when they held a cold pack against their forehead。"},{n:9,type:"gap",q:"Contagious yawning may be connected with ______.",answer:["empathy"],explain:"D 段：contagious yawning is linked to empathy。linked to ≈ connected with。"},{n:10,type:"gap",q:"Yawns spread more easily between family members and close ______.",answer:["friends"],explain:"D 段：stronger between family members and close friends than between strangers。"}]}]},{id:"library",title:"The Library Reinvented",topic:"社会和教育",minutes:15,level:"base",paragraphs:[["A","When the internet became widely available in the late 1990s, many people predicted that public libraries would disappear. If anyone could find information online for free, they asked, why would anyone visit a building full of printed books? In some countries, a number of small libraries did close because of reduced funding."],["B","Yet in many places libraries have survived by changing what they offer. Modern libraries lend e-books and audiobooks as well as paper books, and they provide free computers and wifi for people who cannot afford them at home. For job seekers, this access can be essential, as most job applications must now be completed online."],["C","Libraries have also become community spaces. They run reading groups for children, language classes for newcomers and workshops on topics from coding to gardening. Some even lend unusual items such as musical instruments, tools and sports equipment. In Helsinki, the central library, which opened in 2018, has recording studios, sewing machines and 3D printers that the public can use free of charge."],["D","Visitor numbers suggest that this approach works. Helsinki's central library received more than three million visits in its first year, in a city of about 650,000 people. Librarians report that many visitors come to study, to meet friends or simply to sit somewhere warm and quiet without having to buy anything."],["E","However, librarians warn that these new services depend on stable funding. Buying e-book licences is often more expensive than buying printed copies, because publishers charge libraries more for digital versions, and some licences must be renewed every few years."]],groups:[{title:"Questions 1–3",instruction:"Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Choose TRUE, FALSE or NOT GIVEN.",method:"reading-2",questions:[{n:1,type:"tfng",q:"Some people expected libraries to close because of the internet.",answer:"TRUE",explain:"A 段：many people predicted that public libraries would disappear。predicted ≈ expected，disappear ≈ close，选 TRUE。"},{n:2,type:"tfng",q:"Most job applications today have to be made online.",answer:"TRUE",explain:"B 段：most job applications must now be completed online。选 TRUE。"},{n:3,type:"tfng",q:"Helsinki's central library charges a small fee for using its 3D printers.",answer:"FALSE",explain:"C 段：3D printers that the public can use free of charge。免费，和题目说收费相反，选 FALSE。"}]},{title:"Questions 4–7",instruction:"Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.",method:"reading-5",questions:[{n:4,type:"gap",q:"Libraries help people without internet at home by offering free computers and ______.",answer:["wifi"],explain:"B 段：they provide free computers and wifi for people who cannot afford them at home。"},{n:5,type:"gap",q:"Newcomers to a country can join ______ at the library.",answer:["language classes"],explain:"C 段：language classes for newcomers。"},{n:6,type:"gap",q:"Helsinki's central library opened in ______.",answer:["2018"],explain:"C 段：the central library, which opened in 2018。"},{n:7,type:"gap",q:"Number of visits in the first year: more than ______ million",answer:["three","3"],explain:"D 段：more than three million visits in its first year。写 three 或 3 都可以。"}]},{title:"Questions 8–10",instruction:"Choose the correct letter, A, B, C or D.",method:"reading-13",questions:[{n:8,type:"mcq",q:"Which of the following items is mentioned as something libraries lend?",options:["A  bicycles","B  musical instruments","C  laptops","D  cameras"],answer:"B",explain:"C 段：lend unusual items such as musical instruments, tools and sports equipment。选 B，其他三样文中没提。"},{n:9,type:"mcq",q:"According to paragraph D, why do some people visit libraries?",options:["A  to buy cheap books","B  to find a quiet place without spending money","C  to meet librarians","D  to attend paid courses"],answer:"B",explain:"D 段：to sit somewhere warm and quiet without having to buy anything。选 B。"},{n:10,type:"mcq",q:"Why are e-books a problem for library budgets?",options:["A  Few people want to borrow them.","B  They break easily.","C  Publishers charge libraries higher prices for them.","D  They take up a lot of space."],answer:"C",explain:"E 段：publishers charge libraries more for digital versions。选 C。"}]}]}],a=[{id:"adultlang",title:"Is It Too Late to Learn a Language?",topic:"语言和学习",minutes:15,level:"base",paragraphs:[["A",'Many adults believe that they are too old to learn a new language. They point to children, who seem to pick up languages without effort, and conclude that there is a "critical period" after which learning becomes almost impossible.'],["B","Research shows that the truth is more complicated. Children who move to a new country do usually end up speaking the language with a native accent, while adults often keep a foreign accent for life. In this respect, starting early is a clear advantage."],["C","In the first months of learning, however, adults and teenagers often make faster progress than young children. They have better memories for vocabulary, they can understand grammar rules when these are explained, and they know how to use dictionaries and other study tools. Children usually overtake them only after several years, and mainly because they spend so much more time hearing and using the language."],["D","Motivation also matters. Adults who need a language for work or study, or who want to talk to a partner's family, tend to practise more and give up less often. One survey of adult learners found that those with a clear personal goal were twice as likely to continue their course for more than a year."],["E","Experts therefore recommend that adult learners focus on what they do well. Learning useful phrases, reviewing new words regularly and speaking with real people as early as possible can all help. A foreign accent, they add, is rarely a problem as long as the speaker can be understood easily."]],groups:[{title:"Questions 1–4",instruction:"Do the following statements agree with the views of the writer? Choose YES, NO or NOT GIVEN.",method:"reading-2",questions:[{n:1,type:"ynng",q:"It is impossible for adults to learn a new language.",answer:"NO",explain:"B 段：the truth is more complicated，后文还说成年人前几个月进步更快。作者不同意「成年人学不会」，选 NO。"},{n:2,type:"ynng",q:"Children who start early have an advantage in pronunciation.",answer:"YES",explain:"B 段：孩子最后口音像本地人，In this respect, starting early is a clear advantage。选 YES。"},{n:3,type:"ynng",q:"Adults should study grammar before vocabulary.",answer:"NOT GIVEN",explain:"E 段的建议是学有用的短语、定期复习单词、尽早开口，没说先学语法还是先学单词。选 NOT GIVEN。"},{n:4,type:"ynng",q:"Having a foreign accent is usually a serious problem.",answer:"NO",explain:"E 段：A foreign accent … is rarely a problem as long as the speaker can be understood easily。rarely ≈ 很少，和 usually a serious problem 相反，选 NO。"}]},{title:"Questions 5–7",instruction:"Choose the correct letter, A, B, C or D.",method:"reading-13",questions:[{n:5,type:"mcq",q:"According to paragraph C, why do adults learn faster at first?",options:["A  They are less afraid of making mistakes.","B  They can use study tools and understand rules.","C  They spend more hours on the language.","D  They have better pronunciation."],answer:"B",explain:"C 段：they can understand grammar rules … they know how to use dictionaries and other study tools。选 B。C 是孩子最后超过成年人的原因，正好相反。"},{n:6,type:"mcq",q:"Why do children eventually overtake adults?",options:["A  They have better memories.","B  Their teachers are more experienced.","C  They hear and use the language much more.","D  They study grammar more carefully."],answer:"C",explain:"C 段最后：mainly because they spend so much more time hearing and using the language。选 C。"},{n:7,type:"mcq",q:"What did the survey of adult learners find?",options:["A  Learners with a clear goal were more likely to continue.","B  Most learners gave up within a month.","C  Learners who travelled abroad did better.","D  Older learners were more motivated than younger ones."],answer:"A",explain:"D 段：those with a clear personal goal were twice as likely to continue their course for more than a year。选 A。"}]},{title:"Questions 8–10",instruction:"Complete the summary using the list of words, A–F, below.",method:"reading-4",box:["A  accent","B  regularly","C  rules","D  early","E  goal","F  slowly"],questions:[{n:8,type:"match",q:"Adult learners are advised to review new words ______,",options:["A","B","C","D","E","F"],answer:"B",explain:"E 段：reviewing new words regularly。选 B。"},{n:9,type:"match",q:"and to speak with real people as ______ as possible.",options:["A","B","C","D","E","F"],answer:"D",explain:"E 段：speaking with real people as early as possible。选 D。"},{n:10,type:"match",q:"Keeping a foreign ______ is acceptable if listeners can understand the speaker.",options:["A","B","C","D","E","F"],answer:"A",explain:"E 段：A foreign accent … is rarely a problem as long as the speaker can be understood easily。选 A。"}]}]},{id:"fog",title:"Catching Water from Fog",topic:"环境和科技",minutes:15,level:"base",paragraphs:[["A","In some of the driest places on Earth, it almost never rains, yet the air is often full of fog. Along the coast of Chile and Peru, for example, thick fog rolls in from the Pacific Ocean on most mornings. For centuries, local people have noticed that trees in these areas collect drops of water on their leaves."],["B",'Since the 1980s, engineers have tried to copy this process with "fog nets". These are large sheets of fine plastic mesh stretched between two poles and placed on hillsides facing the wind. As fog passes through the mesh, tiny droplets stick to it, join together and run down into a pipe at the bottom.'],["C","A single net of about 40 square metres can collect several hundred litres of water on a foggy day. The water is usually clean enough to drink after simple filtering, and it can also be used to grow vegetables or to give to animals. The nets need no electricity and are cheap to build."],["D","Fog nets have now been installed in more than a dozen countries, including Morocco, Eritrea and Nepal. In Morocco, a project in the mountains supplies water to several villages, so that women and girls no longer have to spend hours each day walking to distant wells."],["E","The technology does have limits. It only works in places where fog is frequent and the wind blows in the right direction, and nets can be damaged by strong storms. Researchers are now testing new materials inspired by a desert beetle whose back is covered in bumps that collect water from the air."]],groups:[{title:"Questions 1–4",instruction:"Complete the flow chart below. Choose ONE WORD ONLY from the passage for each answer.",method:"reading-5",questions:[{n:1,type:"gap",q:"Fog nets are made of fine plastic ______.",answer:["mesh"],explain:"B 段：large sheets of fine plastic mesh。"},{n:2,type:"gap",q:"They are placed on hillsides that face the ______.",answer:["wind"],explain:"B 段：placed on hillsides facing the wind。"},{n:3,type:"gap",q:"Tiny ______ stick to the net and join together.",answer:["droplets"],explain:"B 段：tiny droplets stick to it, join together。注意复数。"},{n:4,type:"gap",q:"The water runs down into a ______ at the bottom.",answer:["pipe"],explain:"B 段：run down into a pipe at the bottom。"}]},{title:"Questions 5–8",instruction:"Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Choose TRUE, FALSE or NOT GIVEN.",method:"reading-2",questions:[{n:5,type:"tfng",q:"Local people in Chile noticed that trees collected water from fog.",answer:"TRUE",explain:"A 段：local people have noticed that trees in these areas collect drops of water on their leaves。选 TRUE。"},{n:6,type:"tfng",q:"Fog nets need electricity to work.",answer:"FALSE",explain:"C 段：The nets need no electricity。选 FALSE。"},{n:7,type:"tfng",q:"Water from fog nets must be boiled before people can drink it.",answer:"FALSE",explain:"C 段：The water is usually clean enough to drink after simple filtering。只要简单过滤，不是必须煮沸。原文给了相反的信息，选 FALSE。"},{n:8,type:"tfng",q:"The project in Morocco was paid for by the government.",answer:"NOT GIVEN",explain:"D 段只说项目给几个村子供水，没说谁出的钱。选 NOT GIVEN。"}]},{title:"Questions 9–10",instruction:"Choose TWO letters, A–E. Which TWO limits of fog nets are mentioned in the passage?",method:"reading-13",box:["A  They can be damaged in storms.","B  They are expensive to build.","C  They need fog and wind from the right direction.","D  The water tastes of plastic.","E  Animals often destroy them."],questions:[{n:9,type:"match",q:"First limit",options:["A","B","C","D","E"],answer:["A","C"],explain:"E 段：It only works in places where fog is frequent and the wind blows in the right direction（C），and nets can be damaged by strong storms（A）。两题答 A、C，顺序不限。B 和原文 cheap to build 相反。"},{n:10,type:"match",q:"Second limit",options:["A","B","C","D","E"],answer:["A","C"],explain:"同上：A 和 C，顺序不限，但两题不能填同一个字母。"}]}]}],n=[{id:"coral",title:"The Hidden Life of Coral Reefs",topic:"海洋和生态",minutes:20,level:"exam",paragraphs:[["A","Coral reefs cover less than one percent of the ocean floor, yet they are home to roughly a quarter of all known marine species. For this reason they are often described as the rainforests of the sea. Although a reef may look like a mass of colourful rock, it is in fact built by living animals. Each coral is a colony of tiny creatures called polyps, which are related to jellyfish. Over thousands of years, the polyps produce hard skeletons of calcium carbonate, and these skeletons gradually build up into the vast structures that divers see today."],["B","The success of reef-building corals depends on a remarkable partnership. Inside the tissue of each polyp live microscopic algae known as zooxanthellae. Like plants, the algae use sunlight to produce sugars, and they pass most of these sugars to the coral. In return, the coral provides the algae with shelter and with the carbon dioxide they need. The algae also give corals much of their colour. Because the algae need light, reef-building corals are mostly found in clear, shallow water, usually less than 50 metres deep."],["C","This partnership is, however, easily disturbed. When the water becomes too warm, even by one or two degrees above the normal summer maximum, the algae begin to produce harmful chemicals and the coral expels them. Without the algae, the coral loses its colour and turns white, a process known as bleaching. A bleached coral is not dead, and if temperatures return to normal within a few weeks, the algae can return. If the heat continues, though, the coral slowly starves. The Great Barrier Reef in Australia experienced mass bleaching in 2016, 2017, 2020 and 2022, and scientists estimate that around half of its shallow-water corals died during the first two events alone."],["D","Rising temperatures are not the only threat. As the oceans absorb carbon dioxide from the atmosphere, the water becomes more acidic, which makes it harder for corals to build their skeletons. Pollution from farms can also cause problems. Fertilisers washed into the sea encourage the growth of seaweed, which competes with corals for space, and they also support large numbers of crown-of-thorns starfish, which feed on coral polyps. Overfishing adds to the damage by removing the fish that normally keep seaweed under control."],["E","Losing reefs would have serious consequences for people as well as for wildlife. Hundreds of millions of people depend on reef fish for food or income, and reefs attract tourists who spend billions of dollars each year. Reefs also act as natural barriers. By breaking up waves before they reach the shore, they protect coastal towns from storms and reduce erosion. One study calculated that without reefs, the cost of flood damage around the world would double."],["F",'Scientists are now exploring ways to help corals survive. Some are growing young corals in underwater nurseries and then attaching them to damaged reefs, a method sometimes compared to replanting a forest. Others are searching for corals that naturally tolerate higher temperatures, with the aim of breeding more heat-resistant colonies. In laboratories, researchers have even exposed corals to slowly rising temperatures to see whether they can be "trained" to cope with heat.'],["G","Most marine biologists, however, warn that these projects can only buy time. Restoration is expensive and can cover only small areas: replanting a single hectare of reef may cost hundreds of thousands of dollars, while the Great Barrier Reef alone covers about 34 million hectares. In their view, the only long-term solution is to reduce the greenhouse gas emissions that are warming the oceans. Without that, they argue, even the most careful restoration work will eventually be lost."]],groups:[{title:"Questions 1–5",instruction:"Choose the correct heading for paragraphs B–F from the list of headings below.",method:"reading-8",box:["i  How reefs benefit human communities","ii  A relationship that helps both partners","iii  The first scientific study of reefs","iv  What happens when the sea gets too hot","v  Attempts to repair and protect reefs","vi  Several other dangers facing reefs","vii  Why reefs are found near the equator","viii  The cost of tourism to reefs"],questions:[{n:1,type:"heading",q:"Paragraph B",options:["i","ii","iii","iv","v","vi","vii","viii"],answer:"ii",explain:"B 段讲珊瑚和藻类的合作：藻类给珊瑚糖分，珊瑚给藻类住所和二氧化碳（In return）。互惠关系，选 ii。"},{n:2,type:"heading",q:"Paragraph C",options:["i","ii","iii","iv","v","vi","vii","viii"],answer:"iv",explain:"C 段讲水温过高导致白化（bleaching）。选 iv。"},{n:3,type:"heading",q:"Paragraph D",options:["i","ii","iii","iv","v","vi","vii","viii"],answer:"vi",explain:"D 段开头 Rising temperatures are not the only threat，接着讲酸化、农业污染、过度捕捞。其他威胁，选 vi。"},{n:4,type:"heading",q:"Paragraph E",options:["i","ii","iii","iv","v","vi","vii","viii"],answer:"i",explain:"E 段讲珊瑚礁对人的作用：食物收入、旅游、防浪。选 i。viii 是干扰项：文中说游客带来收入，没说旅游的代价。"},{n:5,type:"heading",q:"Paragraph F",options:["i","ii","iii","iv","v","vi","vii","viii"],answer:"v",explain:"F 段讲培育珊瑚、寻找耐热珊瑚等帮助珊瑚存活的办法。选 v。"}]},{title:"Questions 6–9",instruction:"Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Choose TRUE, FALSE or NOT GIVEN.",method:"reading-2",questions:[{n:6,type:"tfng",q:"Coral polyps are closely related to plants.",answer:"FALSE",explain:"A 段：polyps, which are related to jellyfish。珊瑚虫和水母是亲戚，是动物，不是植物。原文给了不同的信息，选 FALSE。"},{n:7,type:"tfng",q:"Most reef-building corals live in water deeper than 50 metres.",answer:"FALSE",explain:"B 段：usually less than 50 metres deep。选 FALSE。"},{n:8,type:"tfng",q:"A coral that has turned white has already died.",answer:"FALSE",explain:"C 段：A bleached coral is not dead。选 FALSE。"},{n:9,type:"tfng",q:"Bleaching on the Great Barrier Reef was worse in 2022 than in 2016.",answer:"NOT GIVEN",explain:"C 段列了 2016、2017、2020、2022 四次白化，只说前两次死了约一半，没有比较 2022 和 2016 哪次更严重。选 NOT GIVEN。"}]},{title:"Questions 10–13",instruction:"Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.",method:"reading-3",questions:[{n:10,type:"gap",q:"Coral skeletons are made of ______.",answer:["calcium carbonate"],explain:"A 段：hard skeletons of calcium carbonate。"},{n:11,type:"gap",q:"Fertilisers from farms help ______ to grow, which then compete with corals for space.",answer:["seaweed"],explain:"D 段：Fertilisers … encourage the growth of seaweed, which competes with corals for space。"},{n:12,type:"gap",q:"Crown-of-thorns starfish damage reefs by eating ______.",answer:["coral polyps","polyps"],explain:"D 段：crown-of-thorns starfish, which feed on coral polyps。feed on ≈ eating。"},{n:13,type:"gap",q:"Young corals are grown in underwater ______ before being moved to damaged reefs.",answer:["nurseries"],explain:"F 段：growing young corals in underwater nurseries and then attaching them to damaged reefs。"}]}]},{id:"placebo",title:"The Power of the Placebo",topic:"医学和心理",minutes:20,level:"exam",paragraphs:[["A","A placebo is a treatment that contains no active ingredient, such as a sugar pill or an injection of salt water. For much of the twentieth century, doctors regarded placebos mainly as a tool for testing new medicines. In a typical trial, one group of patients receives the real drug and another receives a placebo that looks identical. Neither the patients nor the doctors know who has received which, so that their expectations cannot influence the results. A drug is considered effective only if it performs clearly better than the placebo."],["B","What surprised researchers was how often patients in the placebo groups improved. In trials of treatments for pain, depression and some digestive problems, between a third and a half of patients taking placebos reported feeling significantly better. At first, many scientists assumed that these patients would have recovered anyway, or that they were simply telling researchers what they wanted to hear. Later studies, however, showed that placebos can produce measurable changes in the body. Brain scans, for example, have shown that placebo painkillers can trigger the release of endorphins, the body's own natural painkillers."],["C","The strength of the placebo effect depends on surprising details. Two placebo pills tend to work better than one, and injections usually work better than pills. Colour also seems to matter: in several studies, blue pills were more effective as sleeping tablets, while red pills worked better as stimulants. Price plays a role too. In one experiment, volunteers who were told that a painkiller cost $2.50 per pill reported much greater pain relief than those told that the same pill had been reduced to 10 cents."],["D","The relationship between doctor and patient is also important. In a study of patients with irritable bowel syndrome, all participants received fake acupuncture, but some were treated by a practitioner who was warm and attentive and who spent 45 minutes with them, while others had a brief and businesslike appointment. Sixty-two percent of the first group reported improvement, compared with forty-four percent of the second."],["E",'Perhaps the most unexpected finding is that placebos can work even when patients know they are taking one. In 2010, researchers at Harvard gave patients a bottle of pills clearly labelled "placebo" and explained that the pills contained no medicine. Even so, after three weeks the patients reported twice as much improvement as a group that received no treatment. Similar "open-label" studies have since produced positive results for back pain and tiredness, although most have involved small numbers of patients.'],["F","Placebos also have a darker side, known as the nocebo effect. When patients expect a treatment to cause harm, they are more likely to experience side effects. In trials, people taking placebos often report headaches, sickness or tiredness, especially if they have been warned that the real drug may cause these problems. Some researchers believe that a significant proportion of the side effects reported by patients are caused by expectation rather than by the medicine itself."],["G","Doctors remain divided about how this knowledge should be used. Prescribing a placebo without telling the patient is generally considered dishonest, and placebos cannot cure infections or shrink tumours. Yet many argue that the findings have a practical lesson for all medicine: the way a treatment is delivered, and the words a doctor chooses, may affect how well it works."]],groups:[{title:"Questions 1–5",instruction:"Which paragraph contains the following information? Choose the correct letter, A–G.",method:"reading-9",questions:[{n:1,type:"match",q:"an explanation of why trials are designed so that nobody knows who receives the real drug",options:["A","B","C","D","E","F","G"],answer:"A",explain:"A 段：Neither the patients nor the doctors know who has received which, so that their expectations cannot influence the results。选 A。"},{n:2,type:"match",q:"evidence that a placebo can cause a physical response in the brain",options:["A","B","C","D","E","F","G"],answer:"B",explain:"B 段：Brain scans … placebo painkillers can trigger the release of endorphins。选 B。"},{n:3,type:"match",q:"a comparison between two styles of treatment by a practitioner",options:["A","B","C","D","E","F","G"],answer:"D",explain:"D 段：热情、45 分钟的治疗 vs 简短公事公办的治疗。选 D。"},{n:4,type:"match",q:"a reason why giving placebos secretly may be wrong",options:["A","B","C","D","E","F","G"],answer:"G",explain:"G 段：Prescribing a placebo without telling the patient is generally considered dishonest。without telling ≈ secretly，选 G。"},{n:5,type:"match",q:"a reference to the effect of how expensive a treatment seems",options:["A","B","C","D","E","F","G"],answer:"C",explain:"C 段：Price plays a role too，$2.50 和 10 cents 的实验。选 C。"}]},{title:"Questions 6–9",instruction:"Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Choose TRUE, FALSE or NOT GIVEN.",method:"reading-2",questions:[{n:6,type:"tfng",q:"Early researchers thought placebo patients might have got better without any treatment.",answer:"TRUE",explain:"B 段：many scientists assumed that these patients would have recovered anyway。recovered anyway ≈ got better without any treatment，选 TRUE。"},{n:7,type:"tfng",q:"Red pills were found to work better than blue pills as sleeping tablets.",answer:"FALSE",explain:"C 段：blue pills were more effective as sleeping tablets, while red pills worked better as stimulants。选 FALSE。"},{n:8,type:"tfng",q:"The patients in the 2010 Harvard study were told that their pills contained no medicine.",answer:"TRUE",explain:"E 段：explained that the pills contained no medicine。选 TRUE。"},{n:9,type:"tfng",q:"Open-label placebos have been tested on thousands of patients with back pain.",answer:"FALSE",explain:"E 段：most have involved small numbers of patients。说的是人数少，和「几千人」相反，选 FALSE。"}]},{title:"Questions 10–13",instruction:"Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD ONLY from the passage for each answer.",method:"reading-4",questions:[{n:10,type:"gap",q:"In general, ______ produce a stronger placebo effect than pills.",answer:["injections"],explain:"C 段：injections usually work better than pills。"},{n:11,type:"gap",q:"When patients expect harm, they may suffer side effects; this is called the ______ effect.",answer:["nocebo"],explain:"F 段：Placebos also have a darker side, known as the nocebo effect。"},{n:12,type:"gap",q:"Patients are especially likely to report side effects if they have been ______ about them.",answer:["warned"],explain:"F 段：especially if they have been warned that the real drug may cause these problems。"},{n:13,type:"gap",q:"Placebos cannot cure ______ or shrink tumours.",answer:["infections"],explain:"G 段：placebos cannot cure infections or shrink tumours。"}]}]},{id:"navigation",title:"Finding the Way Across the Pacific",topic:"历史和探索",minutes:20,level:"exam",paragraphs:[["A","Between about 3,000 and 800 years ago, the ancestors of today's Polynesians settled almost every habitable island in a vast triangle of the Pacific Ocean, stretching from Hawaii in the north to New Zealand in the south-west and Easter Island in the south-east. Some of these islands lie thousands of kilometres from the nearest land. When European explorers arrived in the eighteenth century, they were astonished to find people living on such remote islands and could not understand how they had got there without compasses, charts or metal tools."],["B","For a long time, many Western scholars believed that the islands must have been settled by accident. According to this view, fishing canoes were blown off course by storms and happened to land on new islands. In the 1950s, a writer named Andrew Sharp argued that deliberate long-distance navigation was impossible without instruments. His ideas were widely accepted, even though Polynesian oral histories described planned voyages in detail."],["C","The accident theory was eventually challenged by practical experiment. In 1976, a double-hulled voyaging canoe called Hōkūleʻa sailed from Hawaii to Tahiti, a distance of more than 4,000 kilometres, without any modern instruments. The navigator, Mau Piailug, came from a small island in Micronesia where traditional navigation skills were still taught. The voyage took 34 days, and the canoe arrived exactly where it had intended to go. Since then, Hōkūleʻa has completed many more voyages, including a journey around the world that ended in 2017."],["D","Traditional navigators rely on a detailed knowledge of the sky. They memorise the points on the horizon where dozens of stars rise and set, and they use these points as a kind of compass. As one star climbs too high to be useful, the navigator switches to another star rising at the same point. During the day, the position of the sun is used in a similar way, although it is less precise."],["E","The ocean itself also provides information. Experienced navigators can identify the direction of different swells, the long, regular waves produced by distant weather systems, and they can feel changes in the swell pattern through the movement of the canoe. Some navigators are said to have lain down in the hull of the canoe in order to feel these movements more clearly."],["F","As a canoe approaches land, other signs become important. Certain seabirds, such as terns and noddies, fly out to sea in the morning to fish and return to land at night, so their flight path at dusk points towards an island. Clouds often gather above islands and may appear slightly green when they reflect a lagoon below. Floating plants and changes in the colour of the water can also show that land is near. Using these signs, navigators effectively increase the size of their target, since they only need to come within about 50 kilometres of an island to detect it."],["G","Today, traditional navigation is being taught again in Hawaii, New Zealand and elsewhere. For many Pacific Islanders, the revival is about more than sailing. It has restored pride in a body of knowledge that outsiders once dismissed, and young navigators are now using the voyages to raise awareness of ocean pollution and climate change, which threaten many low-lying islands."]],groups:[{title:"Questions 1–4",instruction:"Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Choose TRUE, FALSE or NOT GIVEN.",method:"reading-2",questions:[{n:1,type:"tfng",q:"European explorers were surprised to find people on remote Pacific islands.",answer:"TRUE",explain:"A 段：they were astonished to find people living on such remote islands。astonished ≈ surprised，选 TRUE。"},{n:2,type:"tfng",q:"Andrew Sharp's ideas were rejected by most scholars at the time.",answer:"FALSE",explain:"B 段：His ideas were widely accepted。选 FALSE。"},{n:3,type:"tfng",q:"Mau Piailug had learned navigation from his grandfather.",answer:"NOT GIVEN",explain:"C 段只说他来自一个仍在教传统航海的小岛，没说跟谁学的。选 NOT GIVEN。"},{n:4,type:"tfng",q:"Hōkūleʻa reached its intended destination on the 1976 voyage.",answer:"TRUE",explain:"C 段：the canoe arrived exactly where it had intended to go。选 TRUE。"}]},{title:"Questions 5–8",instruction:"Complete the table below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.",method:"reading-5",questions:[{n:5,type:"gap",q:"Stars: navigators remember the points on the ______ where stars rise and set.",answer:["horizon"],explain:"D 段：They memorise the points on the horizon where dozens of stars rise and set。"},{n:6,type:"gap",q:"Ocean: navigators feel the pattern of ______ through the canoe's movement.",answer:["swells","the swell","swell"],explain:"E 段：identify the direction of different swells … feel changes in the swell pattern through the movement of the canoe。"},{n:7,type:"gap",q:"Birds: at dusk, their flight path shows the direction of an ______.",answer:["island"],explain:"F 段：their flight path at dusk points towards an island。空前是 an，要填元音开头的单数名词。"},{n:8,type:"gap",q:"Navigators only need to get within about ______ kilometres of an island to detect it.",answer:["50","fifty"],explain:"F 段：they only need to come within about 50 kilometres of an island to detect it。"}]},{title:"Questions 9–11",instruction:"Choose the correct letter, A, B, C or D.",method:"reading-13",questions:[{n:9,type:"mcq",q:"What did the 1976 voyage of Hōkūleʻa show?",options:["A  that canoes were faster than European ships","B  that long voyages without instruments were possible","C  that Polynesians came from South America","D  that storms carried canoes to new islands"],answer:"B",explain:"C 段：The accident theory was eventually challenged … sailed … without any modern instruments … arrived exactly where it had intended。证明不用仪器也能有计划地远航，选 B。D 正是被挑战的「意外理论」。"},{n:10,type:"mcq",q:"Why might a cloud appear slightly green?",options:["A  It is reflecting a lagoon.","B  It contains rain.","C  It is lit by the setting sun.","D  It is close to the sea surface."],answer:"A",explain:"F 段：Clouds … may appear slightly green when they reflect a lagoon below。选 A。"},{n:11,type:"mcq",q:"According to paragraph G, what are young navigators using the voyages for?",options:["A  to find new islands to live on","B  to attract foreign tourists","C  to draw attention to environmental problems","D  to test modern equipment"],answer:"C",explain:"G 段：using the voyages to raise awareness of ocean pollution and climate change。raise awareness ≈ draw attention，选 C。"}]},{title:"Questions 12–13",instruction:"Choose TWO letters, A–E. Which TWO signs of nearby land are mentioned in the passage?",method:"reading-13",box:["A  floating plants","B  the smell of smoke","C  a change in the colour of the water","D  warmer air temperatures","E  the sound of waves on rocks"],questions:[{n:12,type:"match",q:"First sign",options:["A","B","C","D","E"],answer:["A","C"],explain:"F 段：Floating plants and changes in the colour of the water can also show that land is near。答 A 和 C，顺序不限。其他三项文中没提。"},{n:13,type:"match",q:"Second sign",options:["A","B","C","D","E"],answer:["A","C"],explain:"同上：A 和 C，两题不能填同一个字母。"}]}]}],o=[{id:"remote",title:"Working from Home: The Great Experiment",topic:"工作和社会",minutes:20,level:"exam",paragraphs:[["A","Before 2020, working from home was a privilege enjoyed by a small minority. In the United States, for example, only around five percent of working days were spent at home. Many managers believed that employees who could not be seen would not work hard, and few companies had the technology to let entire teams work remotely. Then, within a few weeks in the spring of 2020, millions of office workers around the world were sent home as governments tried to slow the spread of a new virus."],["B","This sudden change became what some economists have called the largest workplace experiment in history. To the surprise of many managers, most businesses continued to function. Video-calling software, which had previously been used mainly for occasional meetings, became part of daily life, and employees quickly learned to share documents and solve problems online. Surveys carried out in 2021 found that a majority of remote workers felt at least as productive at home as they had in the office."],["C","Researchers have since tried to measure productivity more objectively, with mixed results. A well-known study of a Chinese travel company, carried out before the pandemic, found that call-centre staff who worked from home completed thirteen percent more calls, partly because they took fewer breaks and sick days. However, other studies have found that remote workers find it harder to learn from colleagues and that new employees in particular receive less feedback. Work that requires creativity or close cooperation appears to suffer more than routine tasks."],["D","For employees, the most obvious benefit has been the end of the daily commute. One international study estimated that working from home saved the average employee about 72 minutes a day, and that workers spent roughly a third of this extra time on additional work and the rest on leisure and family. Parents valued the flexibility to fit work around school times, and people with disabilities reported that remote work made it easier to manage their health."],["E","There have also been costs. Many people have found it hard to separate work from home life, and some report working longer hours than before. Loneliness is another problem, especially for young workers who live alone or in shared flats with little space for a desk. Some researchers are also concerned that employees who are rarely seen in the office may be overlooked when promotions are decided, a problem that could affect women more than men if women are more likely to choose remote work."],["F","The shift has had effects far beyond individual companies. In city centres, cafés, sandwich shops and dry cleaners that depended on office workers lost much of their business, and the value of office buildings has fallen in many cities. At the same time, some smaller towns have gained new residents who no longer need to live close to their workplace. Housing prices in suburbs and rural areas rose faster than in city centres in several countries during 2020 and 2021."],["G",'Today, the most common arrangement in many countries is a "hybrid" model, in which employees spend two or three days a week in the office and the rest at home. Some large companies have demanded that staff return full-time, but they have often met strong resistance, and some employees have changed jobs rather than give up their flexibility. Most experts now expect that working patterns will never return completely to what they were before 2020.']],groups:[{title:"Questions 1–5",instruction:"Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Choose TRUE, FALSE or NOT GIVEN.",method:"reading-2",questions:[{n:1,type:"tfng",q:"Before 2020, many managers doubted that employees would work hard at home.",answer:"TRUE",explain:"A 段：Many managers believed that employees who could not be seen would not work hard。选 TRUE。"},{n:2,type:"tfng",q:"Most businesses stopped working when staff were sent home.",answer:"FALSE",explain:"B 段：most businesses continued to function。选 FALSE。"},{n:3,type:"tfng",q:"The study of the Chinese travel company took place during the pandemic.",answer:"FALSE",explain:"C 段：carried out before the pandemic。选 FALSE。注意 before / during 这种时间细节。"},{n:4,type:"tfng",q:"Routine tasks are less affected by remote work than creative tasks.",answer:"TRUE",explain:"C 段：Work that requires creativity or close cooperation appears to suffer more than routine tasks。反过来说就是常规任务受影响较小，选 TRUE。"},{n:5,type:"tfng",q:"Most remote workers prefer to work in cafés rather than at home.",answer:"NOT GIVEN",explain:"F 段提到咖啡馆，但说的是咖啡馆失去了上班族的生意，没说远程员工喜欢去咖啡馆工作。选 NOT GIVEN。"}]},{title:"Questions 6–9",instruction:"Complete the summary below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.",method:"reading-4",questions:[{n:6,type:"gap",q:"Working from home saved the average employee about ______ minutes a day.",answer:["72","seventy-two"],explain:"D 段：saved the average employee about 72 minutes a day。"},{n:7,type:"gap",q:"Around a ______ of this time was spent on extra work.",answer:["third"],explain:"D 段：workers spent roughly a third of this extra time on additional work。"},{n:8,type:"gap",q:"Young people living alone often suffer from ______.",answer:["loneliness"],explain:"E 段：Loneliness is another problem, especially for young workers who live alone。"},{n:9,type:"gap",q:"Remote workers may be overlooked when decisions about ______ are made.",answer:["promotions"],explain:"E 段：may be overlooked when promotions are decided。注意复数。"}]},{title:"Questions 10–13",instruction:"Choose the correct letter, A, B, C or D.",method:"reading-13",questions:[{n:10,type:"mcq",q:"What problem do new employees face when working remotely?",options:["A  They are paid less.","B  They get less feedback.","C  They work fewer hours.","D  They take more sick days."],answer:"B",explain:"C 段：new employees in particular receive less feedback。选 B。D 是干扰项：原文说在家工作的人请病假更少。"},{n:11,type:"mcq",q:"According to paragraph F, what happened to some smaller towns?",options:["A  They lost many shops.","B  Their office buildings lost value.","C  They attracted new residents.","D  Their housing prices fell."],answer:"C",explain:"F 段：some smaller towns have gained new residents。选 C。A、B 说的是市中心。"},{n:12,type:"mcq",q:"How have employees often reacted to demands to return full-time?",options:["A  They have accepted lower salaries.","B  They have opposed them strongly.","C  They have moved closer to the office.","D  They have asked for longer holidays."],answer:"B",explain:"G 段：they have often met strong resistance。选 B。"},{n:13,type:"mcq",q:"Which of the following is the best title for the passage?",options:["A  Why offices will soon disappear","B  How technology was invented for remote work","C  The benefits and costs of a major change in working life","D  A study of a Chinese travel company"],answer:"C",explain:"全文讲在家办公的兴起、好处（D 段）、代价（E 段）、对城市的影响和以后的混合模式。选 C。A 太绝对（文中说是混合模式）；D 只是 C 段的一个例子。全文标题题要选概括全文的，不要选只说一段的。"}]}]},{id:"tea",title:"How Tea Conquered the World",topic:"历史和贸易",minutes:20,level:"exam",paragraphs:[["A","Tea is the most widely consumed drink in the world after water. According to Chinese legend, it was discovered nearly 5,000 years ago when leaves from a wild tea bush blew into a pot of water being boiled for the emperor Shen Nong. Historians treat this story with caution, but there is good evidence that tea was being drunk in south-western China more than 2,000 years ago, at first mainly as a medicine."],["B","By the time of the Tang dynasty, which lasted from 618 to 907, tea had become a popular everyday drink throughout China. In about 760, a writer named Lu Yu produced The Classic of Tea, the first book devoted entirely to the subject, which described how tea should be grown, prepared and served. At that time, tea leaves were usually pressed into hard cakes, which were easy to store and transport. Pieces were broken off and ground into a powder before being mixed with hot water."],["C","Tea travelled from China in several directions. Buddhist monks carried it to Japan, where it became the centre of an elaborate ceremony. Along trade routes to the west, cakes of tea were exchanged for horses from Tibet and Central Asia. Tea reached Europe much later. Dutch traders brought the first shipments to Amsterdam in about 1610, and at first it was so expensive that only the very rich could afford it."],["D","In Britain, tea became fashionable after 1662, when the Portuguese princess Catherine of Braganza married King Charles II and introduced the habit of tea drinking to the royal court. During the following century, the British East India Company built a highly profitable trade in Chinese tea. Because the government placed a heavy tax on it, however, large quantities were smuggled into the country. When the tax was cut sharply in 1784, smuggling almost disappeared and tea became affordable for ordinary families."],["E","Britain's growing demand created a serious problem. China would accept only silver in exchange for its tea, and Britain had little else that China wanted to buy. To reduce the outflow of silver, British merchants began selling opium, grown in India, to Chinese buyers. When the Chinese government tried to stop the trade, two wars followed, in 1839–1842 and 1856–1860, both of which ended in defeat for China."],["F","At the same time, the British were looking for ways to grow tea outside China. In 1848, a Scottish botanist named Robert Fortune travelled into the Chinese countryside in disguise and collected thousands of tea plants and seeds, as well as information about how tea was processed. Many of these plants were taken to the Himalayan region of Darjeeling in India. A native variety of tea had also been found growing in Assam, in north-eastern India, and by the end of the nineteenth century India and Ceylon, now Sri Lanka, had overtaken China as Britain's main suppliers."],["G","In the twentieth century, new inventions changed the way tea was drunk. The tea bag was reportedly invented by accident in about 1908, when an American merchant sent samples in small silk bags and customers put the bags directly into hot water. Today, more than ninety percent of the tea sold in Britain is in bag form. Meanwhile, China has regained its position as the world's largest tea producer, and new styles such as bubble tea, first created in Taiwan in the 1980s, have spread among young people across the globe."]],groups:[{title:"Questions 1–5",instruction:"Which paragraph contains the following information? Choose the correct letter, A–G.",method:"reading-9",questions:[{n:1,type:"match",q:"an example of tea being traded for animals",options:["A","B","C","D","E","F","G"],answer:"C",explain:"C 段：cakes of tea were exchanged for horses。horses ≈ animals，选 C。"},{n:2,type:"match",q:"a reference to someone who hid his identity in order to obtain plants",options:["A","B","C","D","E","F","G"],answer:"F",explain:"F 段：Robert Fortune travelled into the Chinese countryside in disguise and collected thousands of tea plants。in disguise ≈ hid his identity，选 F。"},{n:3,type:"match",q:"the effect of a reduction in tax",options:["A","B","C","D","E","F","G"],answer:"D",explain:"D 段：When the tax was cut sharply in 1784, smuggling almost disappeared and tea became affordable。选 D。"},{n:4,type:"match",q:"a description of an early form in which tea was stored",options:["A","B","C","D","E","F","G"],answer:"B",explain:"B 段：tea leaves were usually pressed into hard cakes, which were easy to store and transport。选 B。"},{n:5,type:"match",q:"a story that historians do not fully trust",options:["A","B","C","D","E","F","G"],answer:"A",explain:"A 段：Historians treat this story with caution。treat with caution ≈ do not fully trust，选 A。"}]},{title:"Questions 6–9",instruction:"Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Choose TRUE, FALSE or NOT GIVEN.",method:"reading-2",questions:[{n:6,type:"tfng",q:"Tea was originally used in China as a medicine.",answer:"TRUE",explain:"A 段：tea was being drunk … at first mainly as a medicine。选 TRUE。"},{n:7,type:"tfng",q:"Lu Yu's book was the first to deal only with tea.",answer:"TRUE",explain:"B 段：the first book devoted entirely to the subject。devoted entirely ≈ deal only with，选 TRUE。"},{n:8,type:"tfng",q:"When tea first arrived in Amsterdam, most people could afford it.",answer:"FALSE",explain:"C 段：it was so expensive that only the very rich could afford it。选 FALSE。"},{n:9,type:"tfng",q:"Catherine of Braganza grew tea in the royal gardens.",answer:"NOT GIVEN",explain:"D 段只说她把喝茶的习惯带进宫廷，没说她种茶。选 NOT GIVEN。"}]},{title:"Questions 10–13",instruction:"Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.",method:"reading-3",questions:[{n:10,type:"gap",q:"China would only accept ______ as payment for its tea.",answer:["silver"],explain:"E 段：China would accept only silver in exchange for its tea。"},{n:11,type:"gap",q:"British merchants sold ______ to China to reduce the loss of silver.",answer:["opium"],explain:"E 段：British merchants began selling opium … to Chinese buyers。"},{n:12,type:"gap",q:"A type of tea plant that grew naturally was found in ______.",answer:["assam"],explain:"F 段：A native variety of tea had also been found growing in Assam。native ≈ grew naturally。地名首字母大小写不影响判分。"},{n:13,type:"gap",q:"The first tea bags were made of ______.",answer:["silk","silk bags"],explain:"G 段：an American merchant sent samples in small silk bags。答 silk 即可。"}]}]},{id:"plastic",title:"Plastic: From Miracle to Menace",topic:"环境和科技",minutes:20,level:"exam",paragraphs:[["A","The first fully synthetic plastic, Bakelite, was invented in 1907 by the Belgian-American chemist Leo Baekeland. It could be shaped when hot and then set hard, did not conduct electricity and was cheap to produce. Bakelite was soon used for telephones, radios and electrical plugs. Over the following decades, chemists developed many other plastics, including nylon, which replaced silk in stockings, and polyethylene, which is still the most widely produced plastic today."],["B","After the Second World War, plastic production grew at an extraordinary rate. Plastics were light, strong and could be made in any colour or shape, and they began to replace wood, glass and metal in thousands of products. Global production rose from around two million tonnes a year in 1950 to more than 400 million tonnes today. Roughly forty percent of this is used for packaging, much of which is thrown away within minutes or hours of being bought."],["C","The qualities that make plastic useful also make it a problem. Because it is designed to last, most plastic does not break down naturally. Instead, sunlight and waves slowly break it into smaller and smaller pieces. Fragments smaller than five millimetres are known as microplastics, and they have now been found almost everywhere scientists have looked, from Arctic ice to the deepest ocean trenches, as well as in drinking water, salt and human blood."],["D","Only a small proportion of plastic is recycled. It has been estimated that of all the plastic ever produced, around nine percent has been recycled, twelve percent has been burned, and the remaining seventy-nine percent has ended up in landfill sites or the natural environment. One reason for the low figure is that there are many different types of plastic, and they cannot easily be recycled together. Packaging made of several layers of different materials, such as some crisp packets, is particularly difficult to process. In addition, recycled plastic is often more expensive than new plastic made from oil."],["E",'Plastic in the sea causes particular concern. Sea turtles mistake plastic bags for jellyfish, and seabirds feed pieces of plastic to their chicks. Large amounts of fishing equipment are lost or abandoned each year, and this "ghost gear" continues to trap fish, seals and other animals for years. Scientists are still studying how microplastics affect marine life, but laboratory experiments suggest that they can reduce growth and reproduction in some small creatures.'],["F","Governments have begun to act. More than a hundred countries have introduced bans or charges on plastic bags. When England introduced a five-pence charge in 2015, the number of single-use bags sold by major supermarkets fell by more than ninety percent within a few years. The European Union has banned several single-use items, including plastic straws and cutlery, and in 2022 the United Nations agreed to begin negotiations on a global treaty to reduce plastic pollution."],["G","Scientists and companies are also searching for alternatives. Some new materials are made from plants such as corn or sugar cane, although many of these break down only in the high temperatures of industrial composting plants and behave like ordinary plastic in the sea. Researchers have also discovered bacteria and insect larvae that can digest certain plastics, and are trying to use the enzymes involved to break plastic down on a large scale. Most experts agree, however, that new technology will not be enough on its own, and that the most effective solution is to use less plastic in the first place."]],groups:[{title:"Questions 1–4",instruction:"Choose the correct heading for paragraphs C–F from the list of headings below.",method:"reading-8",box:["i  Official measures to cut plastic use","ii  Why so little plastic is reused","iii  The invention of nylon","iv  The threat to wildlife in the oceans","v  A material that does not disappear","vi  The rising price of oil","vii  Plastic in the construction industry"],questions:[{n:1,type:"heading",q:"Paragraph C",options:["i","ii","iii","iv","v","vi","vii"],answer:"v",explain:"C 段：most plastic does not break down naturally，只会碎成微塑料，到处都有。不会消失的材料，选 v。"},{n:2,type:"heading",q:"Paragraph D",options:["i","ii","iii","iv","v","vi","vii"],answer:"ii",explain:"D 段：只有约 9% 被回收，并解释原因（种类多、多层包装、回收料更贵）。选 ii。"},{n:3,type:"heading",q:"Paragraph E",options:["i","ii","iii","iv","v","vi","vii"],answer:"iv",explain:"E 段讲海龟、海鸟、幽灵渔具和微塑料对海洋生物的影响。选 iv。"},{n:4,type:"heading",q:"Paragraph F",options:["i","ii","iii","iv","v","vi","vii"],answer:"i",explain:"F 段：Governments have begun to act，塑料袋收费、欧盟禁令、联合国条约。选 i。iii 是 A 段的细节，不是 C–F 任何一段的主旨。"}]},{title:"Questions 5–8",instruction:"Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Choose TRUE, FALSE or NOT GIVEN.",method:"reading-2",questions:[{n:5,type:"tfng",q:"Bakelite was a good conductor of electricity.",answer:"FALSE",explain:"A 段：did not conduct electricity。选 FALSE。"},{n:6,type:"tfng",q:"Nylon is now the most widely produced plastic.",answer:"FALSE",explain:"A 段：polyethylene, which is still the most widely produced plastic today。产量最大的是聚乙烯，不是尼龙，选 FALSE。"},{n:7,type:"tfng",q:"Microplastics have been found in human blood.",answer:"TRUE",explain:"C 段最后：as well as in drinking water, salt and human blood。选 TRUE。"},{n:8,type:"tfng",q:"Most of the plastic ever made has been burned.",answer:"FALSE",explain:"D 段：烧掉的只有 12%，79% 进了填埋场或自然环境。选 FALSE。"}]},{title:"Questions 9–13",instruction:"Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.",method:"reading-5",questions:[{n:9,type:"gap",q:"About ______ percent of plastic is used for packaging.",answer:["forty","40"],explain:"B 段：Roughly forty percent of this is used for packaging。"},{n:10,type:"gap",q:"Lost fishing equipment is called ______.",answer:["ghost gear"],explain:'E 段：this "ghost gear" continues to trap fish。'},{n:11,type:"gap",q:"England's bag charge, introduced in ______, cut sales by over 90 percent.",answer:["2015"],explain:"F 段：When England introduced a five-pence charge in 2015。"},{n:12,type:"gap",q:"The EU has banned plastic straws and ______.",answer:["cutlery"],explain:"F 段：including plastic straws and cutlery。"},{n:13,type:"gap",q:"Some plant-based plastics only break down in industrial ______.",answer:["composting plants"],explain:"G 段：break down only in the high temperatures of industrial composting plants。答案是两个词 composting plants。"}]}]}],s=["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],i=["YES","NO","NOT GIVEN"],l=e=>e.type==="tfng"?s:e.type==="ynng"?i:e.options??[],r=[{id:"bees",title:"The Return of the Urban Bee",topic:"环境和城市",minutes:20,paragraphs:[["A","For most of the twentieth century, cities were seen as hostile places for bees. Gardens were small, roads and buildings covered most of the land, and pesticides were widely used in parks. Beekeepers preferred to keep their hives in the countryside, where flowering crops provided plenty of food."],["B","Since the early 2000s, however, the picture has changed. In London alone, the number of registered hives more than doubled between 2008 and 2013. Many of these hives were placed on the roofs of office buildings, hotels and schools. Some companies installed them to improve their environmental image, while others hoped that employees would enjoy looking after the bees."],["C","Researchers have found that urban bees often produce more honey than their rural relatives. One explanation is diversity. Farmland may be covered with a single crop that flowers for only a few weeks, whereas city parks, private gardens and roadside verges contain hundreds of plant species that bloom at different times from spring to autumn. Cities are also slightly warmer, which allows bees to remain active for longer each day."],["D","Not everyone welcomes the trend. Some ecologists warn that honeybees compete with wild bees for the same flowers. Unlike honeybees, many wild species live alone and travel only short distances, so they are more easily affected when food becomes scarce. A study in Paris suggested that wild bee numbers fell in areas where the density of hives was highest, although the researchers admitted that other factors may have played a part."],["E","As a result, several cities now encourage residents to plant more flowers rather than add more hives. Toronto, for example, has published a list of native plants that support both honeybees and wild bees, and offers free seeds to schools. Supporters of this approach argue that the best way to help pollinators is to give them food, not to increase their numbers."]],groups:[{title:"Questions 1–4",instruction:"Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Choose TRUE, FALSE or NOT GIVEN.",method:"reading-2",questions:[{n:1,type:"tfng",q:"Before 2000, most beekeepers kept their hives outside cities.",answer:"TRUE",explain:"A 段：Beekeepers preferred to keep their hives in the countryside。题目的 outside cities 就是 countryside 的同义替换，意思一致，选 TRUE。"},{n:2,type:"tfng",q:"The number of registered hives in London fell between 2008 and 2013.",answer:"FALSE",explain:"B 段：the number of registered hives more than doubled between 2008 and 2013。原文说翻了一倍多，题目说下降，意思相反，选 FALSE。"},{n:3,type:"tfng",q:"Most of the new hives in London were owned by schools.",answer:"NOT GIVEN",explain:"B 段只说蜂箱放在办公楼、酒店和学校的屋顶上，没说谁拥有，也没说大多数属于学校。原文没提到，选 NOT GIVEN。注意 most 这类限定词，原文没有对应的比例信息时，通常是 NOT GIVEN。"},{n:4,type:"tfng",q:"Urban bees can stay active for more hours each day than rural bees.",answer:"TRUE",explain:"C 段最后一句：Cities are also slightly warmer, which allows bees to remain active for longer each day。stay active ≈ remain active，for more hours ≈ for longer，选 TRUE。"}]},{title:"Questions 5–7",instruction:"Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.",method:"reading-3",questions:[{n:5,type:"gap",q:"Some companies kept bees on their roofs in order to improve their ______.",answer:["environmental image"],explain:"B 段：Some companies installed them to improve their environmental image。答案是两个词 environmental image，拼写要和原文一致。"},{n:6,type:"gap",q:"On farmland, bees may depend on a ______ that flowers for only a short time.",answer:["single crop"],explain:"C 段：Farmland may be covered with a single crop that flowers for only a few weeks。填空前有冠词 a，后面接 that flowers，所以要填名词短语 single crop。"},{n:7,type:"gap",q:"Many species of wild bee live alone and only travel ______.",answer:["short distances"],explain:"D 段：many wild species live alone and travel only short distances。注意 distances 是复数，漏掉 s 算错。"}]},{title:"Questions 8–9",instruction:"Choose the correct letter, A, B, C or D.",method:"reading-13",questions:[{n:8,type:"mcq",q:"What does the writer say about the study in Paris?",options:["A  It proved that hives caused wild bees to disappear.","B  Its authors accepted that other causes were possible.","C  It was later repeated in Toronto.","D  It found that wild bees produce more honey."],answer:"B",explain:"D 段：although the researchers admitted that other factors may have played a part。admitted ≈ accepted，other factors ≈ other causes，选 B。A 说 proved（证明了），原文只用了 suggested，语气太强，是常见陷阱。"},{n:9,type:"mcq",q:"What is the main aim of the approach used in Toronto?",options:["A  to increase the number of hives","B  to pay local beekeepers","C  to provide more food for pollinators","D  to ban pesticides in parks"],answer:"C",explain:"E 段：the best way to help pollinators is to give them food, not to increase their numbers。种花、送种子都是为了给蜜蜂提供食物，选 C。A 正好是原文否定的做法。"}]}]},{id:"words",title:"Why Some Words Survive",topic:"语言和文化",minutes:20,paragraphs:[["A","Every year, dictionary editors add hundreds of new words, from technical terms to slang. Yet only a small proportion of them are still in common use a decade later. Linguists have long asked why some new words become a permanent part of the language while others quickly disappear."],["B",'One popular explanation is that useful words survive because they fill a gap: they name something that had no convenient name before. "Email" is a good example. However, usefulness alone cannot be the whole story. Many perfectly useful words, invented by scientists or advertisers, have never caught on, while some apparently unnecessary words have spread widely.'],["C",'In my view, the most important factor is how easily a word can be used in different situations. The researcher Allan Metcalf has suggested that successful words are often "unobtrusive": people use them without noticing that they are new. Words that draw too much attention to themselves, perhaps because they are too clever or too closely linked to a particular group, tend to be short-lived.'],["D","Frequency also matters, but in a more complicated way than many people assume. A word that is used intensely for a few months, often because of a single news event, may vanish once the event is forgotten. By contrast, words that appear steadily in many different contexts, even at a low level, are more likely to last."],["E","Technology has changed the speed of this process, but not, I believe, its basic rules. Social media allows new expressions to reach millions of people within days, so they rise faster than ever before. Most of them, however, also fall just as quickly. It would be a mistake to conclude that the internet is creating a permanent flood of new vocabulary."]],groups:[{title:"Questions 1–3",instruction:"Which paragraph contains the following information? Choose the correct letter, A–E.",method:"reading-9",questions:[{n:1,type:"match",q:"an example of a word that met a real need",options:["A","B","C","D","E"],answer:"B",explain:'B 段：useful words … fill a gap … "Email" is a good example。fill a gap ≈ met a real need，选 B。'},{n:2,type:"match",q:"a contrast between words used heavily for a short time and words used regularly",options:["A","B","C","D","E"],answer:"D",explain:"D 段：A word that is used intensely for a few months … By contrast, words that appear steadily …。intensely ≈ heavily，steadily ≈ regularly，选 D。"},{n:3,type:"match",q:"a reference to how many words are added to dictionaries each year",options:["A","B","C","D","E"],answer:"A",explain:"A 段第一句：Every year, dictionary editors add hundreds of new words。段落信息匹配题的答案顺序不一定和原文顺序一致，这题就在最前面。"}]},{title:"Questions 4–6",instruction:"Do the following statements agree with the views of the writer? Choose YES, NO or NOT GIVEN.",method:"reading-2",questions:[{n:4,type:"ynng",q:"Usefulness fully explains why new words survive.",answer:"NO",explain:"B 段：usefulness alone cannot be the whole story。作者认为有用并不能完全解释，和题目观点相反，选 NO。YES / NO / NOT GIVEN 问的是作者的观点，判断方法和 TRUE / FALSE / NOT GIVEN 一样。"},{n:5,type:"ynng",q:"Words that are closely associated with one social group are less likely to last.",answer:"YES",explain:"C 段：Words that draw too much attention to themselves, perhaps because they are … too closely linked to a particular group, tend to be short-lived。linked to ≈ associated with，short-lived ≈ less likely to last，选 YES。"},{n:6,type:"ynng",q:"Dictionary editors should add fewer slang words.",answer:"NOT GIVEN",explain:"文中提到词典每年收录俚语，但作者没有评价词典应不应该少收俚语。原文没有这个观点，选 NOT GIVEN。题目里有 should 这类建议词时，要找原文有没有对应的建议。"}]},{title:"Questions 7–9",instruction:"Complete the summary using the list of words, A–G, below.",method:"reading-4",box:["A  unobtrusive","B  faster","C  clever","D  disappear","E  slower","F  useful","G  survive"],questions:[{n:7,type:"match",q:"According to the writer, successful new words are often ______, meaning that people use them without noticing that they are new.",options:["A","B","C","D","E","F","G"],answer:"A",explain:'C 段：successful words are often "unobtrusive": people use them without noticing that they are new。选 A。C clever 是干扰项：原文说太 clever 的词反而活不长。'},{n:8,type:"match",q:"Social media allows new expressions to spread ______ than in the past,",options:["A","B","C","D","E","F","G"],answer:"B",explain:"E 段：so they rise faster than ever before。空后有 than，要填比较级，选 B faster。"},{n:9,type:"match",q:"but most of them also ______ very quickly.",options:["A","B","C","D","E","F","G"],answer:"D",explain:"E 段：Most of them, however, also fall just as quickly。fall ≈ disappear，选 D。G survive 意思相反。"}]}]},{id:"night",title:"Working Through the Night",topic:"健康和工作",minutes:20,paragraphs:[["A","Around one in five employees in industrialised countries works outside normal daytime hours. Nurses, factory workers, pilots and cleaners all keep society running while most people are asleep. But working at night means working against the body's internal clock, and scientists are still learning how high the cost may be."],["B","The human body follows a cycle of roughly 24 hours, known as the circadian rhythm. It is controlled mainly by light: when light enters the eyes in the morning, the brain stops producing melatonin, a hormone that makes us feel sleepy. Night workers therefore try to sleep at a time when their bodies are preparing to be awake."],["C","The short-term effects are well known. Night workers sleep on average one to four hours less than day workers, and their reaction times are slower in the early morning. Several major industrial accidents have happened between midnight and 6 a.m., although it is difficult to prove that tiredness was the main cause in each case."],["D","Long-term risks are harder to measure. Some studies have linked many years of night work with higher rates of heart disease and type 2 diabetes. However, night workers may also differ from day workers in other ways, for instance in diet or income, so researchers must be careful when they interpret the results."],["E","Employers can reduce the harm. Rotating shifts forwards, from morning to afternoon to night, appears to be easier for the body than rotating them backwards. Bright light during the shift and dark rooms for daytime sleep also help. Some hospitals now allow short naps during long night shifts, a practice that was once discouraged."]],groups:[{title:"Questions 1–4",instruction:"Choose the correct heading for paragraphs B–E from the list of headings below.",method:"reading-8",box:["i  Practical steps to protect night workers","ii  Why accidents are always caused by tiredness","iii  The body's natural timetable","iv  Immediate problems caused by working at night","v  Uncertain evidence about long-term health","vi  How much night workers are paid","vii  The history of shift work"],questions:[{n:1,type:"heading",q:"Paragraph B",options:["i","ii","iii","iv","v","vi","vii"],answer:"iii",explain:"B 段讲 circadian rhythm：人体大约 24 小时的周期，由光线控制。natural timetable ≈ cycle of roughly 24 hours，选 iii。"},{n:2,type:"heading",q:"Paragraph C",options:["i","ii","iii","iv","v","vi","vii"],answer:"iv",explain:"C 段第一句是主题句：The short-term effects are well known。short-term ≈ immediate，选 iv。ii 是陷阱：原文说很难证明事故是累造成的，ii 却说 always。"},{n:3,type:"heading",q:"Paragraph D",options:["i","ii","iii","iv","v","vi","vii"],answer:"v",explain:"D 段：Long-term risks are harder to measure … researchers must be careful when they interpret the results。证据不确定，选 v。段落标题题先读每段第一句，往往就是主题句。"},{n:4,type:"heading",q:"Paragraph E",options:["i","ii","iii","iv","v","vi","vii"],answer:"i",explain:"E 段：Employers can reduce the harm，后面列了调班方向、光线、小睡等具体办法，选 i。vi 工资是文中没提到的干扰项。"}]},{title:"Questions 5–6",instruction:"Choose the correct letter, A, B, C or D.",method:"reading-13",questions:[{n:5,type:"mcq",q:"According to paragraph B, melatonin",options:["A  wakes people up in the morning.","B  makes people feel sleepy.","C  is produced only during work.","D  is controlled mainly by food."],answer:"B",explain:"B 段：melatonin, a hormone that makes us feel sleepy。选 B。D 是陷阱：原文说生物钟主要由光线（light）控制，不是食物。"},{n:6,type:"mcq",q:"Why must researchers be careful when they interpret studies of long-term risks?",options:["A  The studies lasted only a few months.","B  Night workers may be different from day workers in other ways.","C  Very few people work at night.","D  Doctors disagree about heart disease."],answer:"B",explain:"D 段：night workers may also differ from day workers in other ways, for instance in diet or income。选 B。C 和 A 段的「五分之一员工上夜班」矛盾。"}]},{title:"Questions 7–8",instruction:"Answer the questions below. Choose NO MORE THAN THREE WORDS from the passage for each answer.",method:"reading-7",questions:[{n:7,type:"gap",q:"What do some hospitals now allow during long night shifts?",answer:["short naps","naps"],explain:"E 段：Some hospitals now allow short naps during long night shifts。答 short naps，不超过三个词。"},{n:8,type:"gap",q:"In which direction is it easier for the body to rotate shifts?",answer:["forwards","forward"],explain:"E 段：Rotating shifts forwards … appears to be easier for the body than rotating them backwards。答 forwards。"}]}]}],h=[...t,...a,...r.map(e=>({...e,level:e.level??"base"})),...n,...o];export{l as optionsOf,h as passages};
